<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- POGIL-style activity, Day 11. Picks up directly after the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus -->
<!-- activity (Activity 10), which is assumed to have been COMPLETED in the previous class -->
<!-- session: students already know that every accumulation function is an antiderivative of -->
<!-- the function it accumulates, and have seen A(x)=x, x^2/2, x^3/3, x^4/4 as the accumulation -->
<!-- functions (hence antiderivatives) of 1, x, x^2, x^3 respectively (from Activity 09) with the -->
<!-- FTC justification for why this always works with a continuous function (from Activity 10). -->

<!-- NOTE: as of writing, source/main.ptx's activities chapter includes only through Activity 09; -->
<!-- Activity 10 (fundamental-theorem-calculus.ptx) exists on disk but was never added to the -->
<!-- include list. Both the Activity 10 and this Activity 11 include are being added together. -->

<!-- This activity does NOT re-derive the FTC or re-justify why accumulation functions are -->
<!-- antiderivatives; that was Activity 10's job. It opens by naming the antiderivative concept -->
<!-- directly (rather than through the accumulation-function limit process), then: -->
<!--   1. generalizes the power-rule pattern already visible in the four known cases to propose -->
<!--      and verify a formula for an antiderivative of x^n (n != -1), -->
<!--   2. notes (briefly) that the accumulation-function definition itself breaks down for -->
<!--      negative powers because of the discontinuity at 0, though the antiderivative formula -->
<!--      is still fine away from 0, -->
<!--   3. has students discover that adding a constant preserves the antiderivative property, and -->
<!--      states (without proof) that any two antiderivatives of the same function on an interval -->
<!--      differ by only a constant, -->
<!--   4. introduces indefinite integral notation, int f(x) dx = F(x)+C, with brief power-rule -->
<!--      practice, -->
<!--   5. points out that the power-rule pattern breaks down at f(x)=x^(-1)=1/x, and -->
<!--   6. uses the FTC to construct ln(x) = int_1^x (1/t) dt as a genuinely new function, ending -->
<!--      on what ln(1) must be and what the domain of ln(x) must be, straight from the -->
<!--      definition. -->

<!-- Deliberately AVOIDS: proving the "antiderivatives differ by a constant" theorem (stated as a -->
<!-- given fact, per instructor's request), giving more than a brief remark on the domain issue -->
<!-- for negative powers (no dedicated task), and any of the algebraic properties of ln (product, -->
<!-- quotient, power rules). Those properties are deliberately postponed to a separate activity; -->
<!-- reaching the definition of ln(x) and these two questions about it is the intended stopping -->
<!-- point for the day, not a shortfall to correct for. -->

<!-- Answer key, for instructor reference (not shown to students): -->
<!--   task-predict-power-pattern: F(x) = x^(n+1)/(n+1), by extending the pattern (exponent goes -->
<!--     up by 1, then divide by the new exponent). -->
<!--   task-verify-power-formula: F'(x) = (n+1)x^n/(n+1) = x^n, confirming the proposal, provided -->
<!--     n+1 != 0, i.e. n != -1. -->
<!--   task-constant-still-antiderivative: G(x)=F(x)+C has G'(x)=F'(x)+0=f(x), so G is also an -->
<!--     antiderivative. There are infinitely many antiderivatives of any f, one for each C. -->
<!--   task-multiple-antideriv-x5: general antiderivative is x^6/6 + C; any three distinct -->
<!--     constants (e.g. x^6/6, x^6/6+2, x^6/6-7) give three different antiderivatives. -->
<!--   task-multiple-antideriv-sqrt: sqrt(x)=x^(1/2), general antiderivative (2/3)x^(3/2)+C. -->
<!--   task-indef-power: int x^4 dx = x^5/5 + C. -->
<!--   task-indef-negative-power: int x^(-3) dx = -x^(-2)/2 + C = -1/(2x^2) + C. -->
<!--   task-indef-radical: int sqrt(x) dx = (2/3)x^(3/2) + C. -->
<!--   task-indef-sum: int (3x^2-5) dx = x^3 - 5x + C. -->
<!--   task-power-rule-breaks: plugging n=-1 into x^(n+1)/(n+1) requires dividing by n+1=0, which -->
<!--     is undefined. The formula simply does not produce anything for n=-1. -->
<!--   task-ln-of-one: ln(1) = int_1^1 (1/t) dt = 0, by the zero-width property (the integral of -->
<!--     any function from a point to itself is 0), with no computation needed. -->
<!--   task-ln-domain: plugging in x=0 asks for int_1^0 (1/t) dt, but 1/t has a discontinuity -->
<!--     (vertical asymptote) at t=0, so this integral does not exist; the same problem occurs for -->
<!--     any negative x, since the path of integration from 1 would have to cross t=0. So the -->
<!--     domain of ln(x) is only x>0, i.e. the interval (0, infinity). -->

<!-- Suggested pacing for a ~50-55 minute class: -->
<!--   Intro + antiderivative definition + recall of four known cases ......... ~5  min -->
<!--   Exercise 1: generalizing the power rule pattern (predict + verify) ..... ~8  min -->
<!--   Remark: domain caveat for negative powers (brief) ...................... ~2  min -->
<!--   Exercise 2: adding a constant; multiple antiderivatives of x^5, sqrt(x)  ~8  min -->
<!--   Theorem (stated) + bridge to indefinite integral notation .............. ~4  min -->
<!--   Exercise 3: indefinite integral practice (4 short tasks) ............... ~10 min -->
<!--   Exercise 4: where the power rule breaks down (x^-1) .................... ~5  min -->
<!--   Remark: FTC constructs a new function; define ln(x) .................... ~3  min -->
<!--   Exercise 5: ln(1) and the domain of ln(x) ............................... ~8  min -->
<!--   TOTAL ..................................................................... ~53 min -->

<!-- Where to cut if you're running short (in order of preference): -->
<!--   1. In Exercise 2, do the x^5 multiple-antiderivatives task in class and assign the sqrt(x) -->
<!--      repeat as follow-up; the pattern is already visible after one example. -->
<!--   2. In Exercise 3, do 2 of the 4 practice tasks in class (keep one pure power and the -->
<!--      radical; drop the negative-power and sum/difference ones) and assign the rest. -->
<!--   3. State the uniqueness theorem and the domain-caveat remark quickly, without lingering -->
<!--      class discussion; both are asides to the day's main thread. -->
<!--   4. Do NOT cut Exercise 1 (generalizing the power rule), Exercise 4 (where the pattern -->
<!--      breaks down at x^-1), or Exercise 5 (defining ln(x) and its two questions). Reaching -->
<!--      "ln(x) is a genuinely new function, defined only for x>0, with ln(1)=0" is the intended -->
<!--      stopping point for the day; the algebraic properties of ln wait for the next activity -->
<!--      regardless of how much of today's material was reached. -->

<worksheet xml:id="activity-antiderivatives-and-natural-log">
  <title>Antiderivatives and a New Kind of Function</title>

  <objectives xml:id="obj-antiderivatives-and-natural-log">
    <ul>
      <li><p>Recall the definition of an <term>antiderivative</term>, and generalize the pattern already seen in known accumulation functions to propose and verify a formula for an antiderivative of <m>f(x)=x^n</m>, for <m>n\ne -1</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Recognize that adding a constant to an antiderivative produces another antiderivative, and that any two antiderivatives of the same function on an interval must differ by a constant.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use indefinite integral notation, <m>\displaystyle\int f(x)\,dx = F(x)+C</m>, to describe the entire family of antiderivatives of <m>f(x)</m> rather than a single one.</p></li>
      <li><p>Identify why the power-rule pattern for antiderivatives breaks down at <m>f(x)=x^{-1}=\frac{1}{x}</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to construct a new function, the <term>natural logarithm</term> <m>\displaystyle\ln(x) = \int_1^x \frac{1}{t}\,dt</m>, as an antiderivative of <m>1/x</m>, and reason directly from this definition about its value at <m>x=1</m> and its domain.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      On the last activity, the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus told you that every accumulation
      function is an antiderivative of the function it accumulates. Today we turn that idea
      around. Instead of starting with a function and building its accumulation function through
      a limit, can we start with a function <m>f(x)</m> and simply propose a formula whose
      derivative is <m>f(x)</m>, directly, with no limit at all?
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <definition xml:id="def-antiderivative">
    <title>Antiderivative</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a function <m>f(x)</m>, another function <m>F(x)</m> is called an
        <term>antiderivative</term> of <m>f(x)</m> if <m>F'(x) = f(x)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark xml:id="rem-known-antiderivatives-recall">
    <title>Recall: Antiderivatives You Have Already Found</title>
    <p>
      Every accumulation function you have built so far is an example:
      <md>
        <mrow>f(x) = 1 \quad \amp \Rightarrow \quad F(x) = x</mrow>
        <mrow>f(x) = x \quad \amp \Rightarrow \quad F(x) = \frac{1}{2}x^2</mrow>
        <mrow>f(x) = x^2 \quad \amp \Rightarrow \quad F(x) = \frac{1}{3}x^3</mrow>
        <mrow>f(x) = x^3 \quad \amp \Rightarrow \quad F(x) = \frac{1}{4}x^4</mrow>
      </md>
      Each of these is a single power of <m>x</m>, but we have not stated a single rule that
      covers every power at once.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-generalize-power-rule" workspace="0in">
    <title>Generalizing the Power Rule</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Look for the pattern connecting each <m>f(x)</m> above to its antiderivative <m>F(x)</m>,
        and use it to make a prediction for a general power <m>f(x)=x^n</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-predict-power-pattern" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What pattern would you predict for an antiderivative of <m>f(x) = x^n</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-verify-power-formula" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write your proposal for <m>F(x)</m> and directly compute <m>F'(x)</m> using derivative
          rules. Is your proposal an antiderivative of <m>x^n</m>? If not, adjust it until
          <m>F'(x)=x^n</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <remark xml:id="rem-domain-caveat-negative-powers">
    <title>A Domain Caveat</title>
    <p>
      Our accumulation functions were built as integrals starting at <m>0</m>. That definition
      breaks down for negative powers, such as <m>f(x)=x^{-2}</m>, because <m>f</m> has a
      discontinuity at <m>x=0</m>: an integral like <m>\int_0^b x^{-2}\,dx</m> simply does not
      exist. The antiderivative formula you just found is still perfectly good as a function in
      its own right, though. It just needs to be considered on an interval that avoids <m>x=0</m>,
      rather than being built as an accumulation function starting from <m>0</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-adding-a-constant" workspace="0in">
    <title>Adding a Constant</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Antiderivatives are not to be unique. This exercise asks you to see why, and to
        practice using the power rule.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-constant-still-antiderivative" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>F(x)</m> is an antiderivative of <m>f(x)</m>, and let <m>C</m> be any
          constant. Form <m>G(x) = F(x)+C</m> and compute <m>G'(x)</m>. Is <m>G(x)</m> also an
          antiderivative of <m>f(x)</m>? How many antiderivatives does a single function
          <m>f(x)</m> actually have?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-multiple-antideriv-x5" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the pattern from the previous exercise, write down at least three different
          antiderivatives of <m>f(x) = x^5</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-multiple-antideriv-sqrt" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Do the same for <m>f(x) = \sqrt{x}</m>. (Rewrite <m>\sqrt{x}</m> as a power of <m>x</m>
          first.)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-antiderivatives-differ-by-constant">
    <title>Antiderivatives Differ Only By a Constant</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>F(x)</m> and <m>G(x)</m> are both antiderivatives of the same function <m>f(x)</m>
        on an interval <m>[a,b]</m>, then <m>F(x)-G(x)</m> is a constant on that interval.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <remark xml:id="rem-antiderivative-family">
    <p>
      Put together with the previous exercise, this theorem says once you know <em>one</em>
      antiderivative <m>F(x)</m> of <m>f(x)</m>, you know all of them: every antiderivative of
      <m>f(x)</m> has the form <m>F(x)+C</m> for some constant <m>C</m>. There are no other
      antiderivatives that aren't captured by this family.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <definition xml:id="def-indefinite-integral">
    <title>The Indefinite Integral</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The notation <m>\displaystyle\int f(x)\,dx</m>, an integral written with no limits of
        integration, is called an <term>indefinite integral</term>. It represents the entire
        <em>family</em> of antiderivatives of <m>f(x)</m>, not a single function and not a number.
        Once one antiderivative <m>F(x)</m> is found, every other antiderivative is obtained by
        adding a constant, so we write
        <md>\int f(x)\,dx = F(x) + C\text{,}</md>
        where <m>C</m>, the <term>constant of integration</term>, is a placeholder for 
        any real number. This is
        different from the definite integral <m>\int_a^b f(x)\,dx</m>, whose limits of
        integration make it a single number.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-indefinite-integral-practice" workspace="0in">
    <title>Practice: Indefinite Integrals</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Use the power-rule formula from Exercise 1 to evaluate each indefinite integral below.
        (The sum rule and constant multiple rule you already know for definite integrals apply
        here too, term by term.)
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-indef-power" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\displaystyle\int x^4\,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-indef-negative-power" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\displaystyle\int x^{-3}\,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-indef-radical" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\displaystyle\int \sqrt{x}\,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-indef-sum" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\displaystyle\int (3x^2-5)\,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-power-rule-breaks" workspace="0in">
    <title>Where the Pattern Breaks Down</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The power rule to find antiderivatives of <m>x^n</m> works with <m>n\ne -1</m>.
        See what happens when <m>n=-1</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-power-rule-breaks" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Try applying your general antiderivative formula to
          <m>f(x) = x^{-1} = \dfrac{1}{x}</m>. What goes wrong? Why can this formula not produce
          an antiderivative of <m>1/x</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <remark xml:id="rem-ftc-creates-new-function">
    <title>A New Kind of Function</title>
    <p>
      The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus does not care whether a function is built from powers
      of <m>x</m>. It only asks for continuity. The function <m>f(t)=1/t</m> is continuous
      everywhere except at <m>t=0</m>, so on any interval avoiding <m>0</m>, it has an
      accumulation function, and that accumulation function must be an antiderivative of
      <m>1/t</m>, whether or not we have an algebraic formula for it in terms of powers. This
      gives us a way to <em>create</em> a genuinely new function, one we have not seen before, as
      an antiderivative of <m>1/x</m>. We name it the <term>natural logarithm</term>:
      <md>\ln(x) = \int_1^x \frac{1}{t}\,dt</md>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-defining-natural-log" workspace="0in">
    <title>Exploring <m>\ln(x)</m></title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        We will be exploring <m>\ln(x)</m> based on its
        definition as an integral. In principle, we don't actually
        know it should be a logarithm, just that it's a valid antiderivative.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-ln-of-one" workspace=".75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is <m>\ln(1)</m>? How is that value forced by the integral itself, with no
          computation needed?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ln-domain" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus requires that the rate function must be
          continuous? Based on this, what must the domain of <m>\ln(x)</m> be?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <remark xml:id="rem-looking-ahead-log-properties">
    <title>Looking Ahead</title>
    <p>
      You now have a genuinely new function, <m>\ln(x)</m>, defined directly by an integral
      rather than by any algebraic formula. In an upcoming activity we will explore its
      other properties.
    </p>
  </remark>

</worksheet>
